The 300-second limit is imposed by Whyline chat, not Codex. In your installed `whyline-relay 0.2.32`, ordinary chat hard-codes `CHAT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS = 300` in [chat.py](/Users/anish/.local/share/uv/tools/whyline/lib/python3.11/site-packages/whyline_relay/chat.py:125). Brainstorm bypasses it by supplying the selected 15/30/45/60-minute timeout.

For your active relay plan, add this near the top of [.whyline/relay/config.toml](/Users/anish/CodeGraph/.whyline/relay/config.toml:1), before any `[section]`:

```toml
plan = "plans/now-i-want-you-to-analyse-the-current-ca.plan.md"
max_rounds = 6
timeout_minutes = 45
```

Then resume after the working tree is safely checkpointed:

```bash
whyline relay resume
```

Do not use `--allow-dirty`. For a completely new run, you can instead use:

```bash
whyline relay start --timeout 45
```

For ordinary `whyline relay chat`, there is currently no supported `--timeout` option. A temporary, non-destructive 60-minute workaround is:

```bash
cd /Users/anish/CodeGraph

/Users/anish/.local/share/uv/tools/whyline/bin/python -c \
'from pathlib import Path; from whyline_relay import chat; chat.CHAT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=3600; chat.repl(Path.cwd())'
```

There is no supported “unlimited” value; do not use `0`, because it would terminate immediately. A large finite duration such as 3600 seconds is safer. OpenAI’s documentation likewise shows that orchestration/client timeouts are configured by the integration around Codex, rather than representing a universal Codex model limit. [Official OpenAI example](https://developers.openai.com/cookbook/examples/codex/codex_mcp_agents_sdk/building_consistent_workflows_codex_cli_agents_sdk)
